"Divides zero-based category bars into additive component segments to show totals and composition together.\n\nUse when: Components are nonnegative, mutually exclusive, and sum to meaningful totals. Total magnitude matters alongside a small number of component contributions.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need accurate comparisons of every interior component across categories.\n\nVisual grammar: Total bar length encodes the category total. Segment length encodes each component value; consistent fill identifies the component.\n\nBuild: Build a complete component table and check its additivity. Choose a stable component order and compute cumulative segment boundaries. Draw zero-based bars and label only segments with sufficient interior space.\n\nCheck: Check component sums equal each category total. Verify component order and colors remain consistent. Confirm categories use the same units and reporting population.\n\nPitfalls: Only the first segment has a shared baseline; interior segments are harder to compare. Use grouped bars or component small multiples when precise component comparisons are the main task.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic expense totals by branch, stacked into rent, staffing, and supplies with labeled total amounts.\n\nAlternatives: 100% stacked bar chart, Grouped bar chart, Treemap."
